## The idea

Scan every cell. Each land cell not seen yet starts a **flood fill** that counts its island. Use an explicit stack (a huge island would overflow recursion) and mark cells as seen **when you push them**, so no cell is counted twice.

## Step by step

1. seen[,] = all false.
2. For each land cell not seen: push it, mark it, area = 0.
3. Pop, area++, push each unseen land neighbour (up, down, left, right), marking it.
4. Keep the largest area.

## Worked example

**Input:** grid 11000 / 11000 / 00110 / 00011

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| cell (0,0) | new island: (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1) → area 4 |
| cells (0,1)…(1,1) | already seen → skip |
| cell (2,2) | new island: (2,2), (2,3), then (3,3) below, then (3,4) → area 4 |
| largest | 4 |

**Result:** 4

## Cost

O(rows × cols) time and memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 695 — the area of the largest island (4-connected 1s) in a grid. Not related to InterviewDB Q3.
public static class IslandArea {
    public static int Max(int[][] grid) {
        if (grid == null || grid.Length == 0) return 0;
        int rows = grid.Length, cols = grid[0].Length, best = 0;
        var seen = new bool[rows, cols];          // do not mark the caller's grid
        int[] dr = { 1, -1, 0, 0 }, dc = { 0, 0, 1, -1 };
        var stack = new Stack<(int, int)>();      // an explicit stack: a 1000x1000 island cannot overflow
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                if (grid[r][c] != 1 || seen[r, c]) continue;
                int area = 0;
                seen[r, c] = true; stack.Push((r, c));
                while (stack.Count > 0) {
                    var (cr, cc) = stack.Pop();
                    area++;
                    for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                        int nr = cr + dr[d], nc = cc + dc[d];
                        if (nr < 0 || nc < 0 || nr >= rows || nc >= cols) continue;
                        if (grid[nr][nc] != 1 || seen[nr, nc]) continue;
                        seen[nr, nc] = true;      // mark when pushed, so no cell is pushed twice
                        stack.Push((nr, nc));
                    }
                }
                if (area > best) best = area;
            }
        return best;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Why mark on push?**

Marking on pop lets the same cell be pushed by up to four neighbours.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(R × C) time and memory for the seen array.

**Q: Land cells arrive one at a time and you report the largest island after each.**

Union-find with a size per root — LeetCode 305 / the "Number of clouds" question in this bank.

**Q: A grid too large for memory.**

Process strips of rows, keeping union-find labels for the boundary row only.

**Q: Why the Q3 warning?**

InterviewDB Q3 "Artificial island" sounds like this but is a 1-D window over an array. Answering with a flood fill would be wrong.
